{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11353","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11353","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11353","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanpei","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Hanpei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sijie","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Sijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nuo","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Nuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai-Xin","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Kai-Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tetsuya","familyName":"Sakai","name":"Sakai, Tetsuya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Do Large Language Models Favor Recent Content? A Study on Recency Bias in LLM-Based Reranking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T17:11:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:49:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-21T16:59:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-22T02:16:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed in information systems, including being used as second-stage rerankers in information retrieval pipelines, yet their susceptibility to recency bias has received little attention. We investigate whether LLMs implicitly favour newer documents by prepending artificial publication dates to passages in the TREC Deep Learning passage retrieval collections in 2021 (DL21) and 2022 (DL22). Across seven models, GPT-3.5-turbo, GPT-4o, GPT-4, LLaMA-3 8B/70B, and Qwen-2.5 7B/72B, \"fresh\" passages are consistently promoted, shifting the Top-10's mean publication year forward by up to 4.78 years and moving individual items by as many as 95 ranks in our listwise reranking experiments. Although larger models attenuate the effect, none eliminate it. We also observe that the preference of LLMs between two passages with an identical relevance level can be reversed by up to 25% on average after date injection in our pairwise preference experiments. These findings provide quantitative evidence of a pervasive recency bias in LLMs and highlight the importance of effective bias-mitigation strategies."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11353","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:00:43Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:00:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-22T04:42:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11252","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11252","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11252","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengze","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chengze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yitong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yitong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liyi","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Liyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ge","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploring the Potential of Diffusion Large Language Models in Code Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T12:51:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:43:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-02T02:15:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-04T01:46:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-13T07:24:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-09-15T01:00:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"LLMs have become the mainstream approaches to code generation. Existing LLMs mainly employ autoregressive generation, i.e. generating code token-by-token from left to right. However, the underlying autoregressive generation has two limitations in code generation. First, autoregressive LLMs only generate a token at each step, showing low efficiency in practice. Second, programming is a non-sequential process involving back-and-forth editing, while autoregressive LLMs only employ the left-to-right generation order. These two intrinsic limitations hinder the further development of LLMs in code generation. Recently, diffusion LLMs have emerged as a promising alternative. Diffusion LLMs address the above limitations with two advances, including multi-token prediction (i.e. generating multiple tokens at each step) and flexible generation order (i.e. flexibly determining which positions to generate tokens). However, there is no systematic study exploring diffusion LLMs in code generation. To bridge the knowledge gap, we present the first empirical study of diffusion LLMs for code generation. Our study involves 9 representative diffusion LLMs and conduct experiments on 4 widely used benchmarks. Based on the results, we summarize the following findings. (1) Existing diffusion LLMs are competitive with autoregressive LLMs with similar sizes. (2) Diffusion LLMs have a stronger length extrapolation ability than autoregressive LLMs and perform better in long code understanding. (3) We explore factors impacting the effectiveness and efficiency of diffusion LLMs, and provide practical guidance. (4) We discuss several promising further directions to improve diffusion LLMs on code generation. We open-source all source code, data, and results to facilitate the following research. The code is publicly available at https://github.com/zhangyitonggg/dllm4code."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ACM TOSEM"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11252","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:58:18Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:58:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-15T04:41:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11250","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11250","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11250","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yitong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yitong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ximo","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ximo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liyi","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Liyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jia","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Environmental Injection Attacks against GUI Agents in Realistic Dynamic Environments"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T12:47:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:43:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-31T08:50:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T01:34:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-24T12:04:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-25T01:35:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Graphical User Interface (GUI) agents are increasingly deployed to interact with online web services, yet their exposure to open-world content renders them vulnerable to Environmental Injection Attacks (EIAs). In these attacks, an attacker can inject crafted triggers into website to manipulate the behavior of GUI agents used by other users. In this paper, we find that most existing EIA studies fall short of realism. In particular, they fail to capture the dynamic nature of real-world web content, often assuming that a trigger's on-screen position and surrounding visual context remain largely consistent between training and testing. To better reflect practice, we introduce a realistic dynamic-environment threat model in which the attacker is a regular user and the trigger is embedded within a dynamically changing environment. Under this threat model, existing approaches largely fail, suggesting that their effectiveness in exposing GUI agent vulnerabilities has been substantially overestimated.\n To expose the hidden vulnerabilities of existing GUI agents effectively, we propose Chameleon, an attack framework with two key novelties designed for dynamic environments. (1) To synthesize more realistic training data, we introduce LLM-Driven Environment Simulation, which automatically generates diverse, high-fidelity webpage simulations that mimic the variability of real-world dynamic environments. (2) To optimize the trigger more effectively, we introduce Attention Black Hole, which converts attention weights into explicit supervisory signals. This mechanism encourages the agent to remain insensitive to irrelevant surrounding content, thereby improving robustness in dynamic environments. We evaluate Chameleon on six realistic websites and four representative LVLM-powered GUI agents, where it significantly outperforms existing methods."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11250","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:58:15Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:58:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T03:49:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10761","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10761","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10761","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcelo","familyName":"Sandoval-Castaneda","name":"Sandoval-Castaneda, Marcelo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bryan","familyName":"Russell","name":"Russell, Bryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Josef","familyName":"Sivic","name":"Sivic, Josef","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gregory","familyName":"Shakhnarovich","name":"Shakhnarovich, Gregory","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabian Caba","familyName":"Heilbron","name":"Heilbron, Fabian Caba","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EditDuet: A Multi-Agent System for Video Non-Linear Editing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T00:27:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-30T00:26:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3721238.3730761","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automated tools for video editing and assembly have applications ranging from filmmaking and advertisement to content creation for social media. Previous video editing work has mainly focused on either retrieval or user interfaces, leaving actual editing to the user. In contrast, we propose to automate the core task of video editing, formulating it as sequential decision making process. Ours is a multi-agent approach. We design an Editor agent and a Critic agent. The Editor takes as input a collection of video clips together with natural language instructions and uses tools commonly found in video editing software to produce an edited sequence. On the other hand, the Critic gives natural language feedback to the editor based on the produced sequence or renders it if it is satisfactory. We introduce a learning-based approach for enabling effective communication across specialized agents to address the language-driven video editing task. Finally, we explore an LLM-as-a-judge metric for evaluating the quality of video editing system and compare it with general human preference. We evaluate our system's output video sequences qualitatively and quantitatively through a user study and find that our system vastly outperforms existing approaches in terms of coverage, time constraint satisfaction, and human preference."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"SIGGRAPH 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10761","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-16T02:46:49Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:46:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-30T02:19:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11076","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11076","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11076","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zibo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yuhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhibin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shipeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinjing","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xinjing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chendong","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Chendong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingxu","familyName":"Mu","name":"Mu, Bingxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqing","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yuqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiheng","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Zhiheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"She","name":"She, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shu","familyName":"You","name":"You, Shu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanghuan","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Guanghuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rong","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Rong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanchun","familyName":"Dou","name":"Dou, Wanchun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guihai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Guihai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SmartSwap: Swap-Based Memory Optimization for LLM Training under Varying Operator Sequences"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T03:55:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:33:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-07-15T13:51:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-16T00:48:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The increasing size of large language models (LLMs) has led to a surge in memory requirements during training, often exceeding the capacity of high-bandwidth memory (HBM). Swap-based memory optimization incurs neither accuracy loss nor additional end-to-end overhead when effectively overlapped, thus being an attractive solution. However, existing swap methods assume consistent operator sequences, which is impractical in Eager Mode, where operator sequences can vary during change.\n We propose Chameleon, which redesigns the end-to-end process of swap-based memory optimization and is the first work to consider varying operator sequences in Eager Mode. Chameleon (i) introduces a lightweight online profiler to enable continuous profiling for monitoring operator sequences, (ii) generates effective swap policies with limited operator information, and (iii) optimizes the policy execution module for accurate policy application and better performance. Experimental results demonstrate that Chameleon reduces profiling overhead by 84.25%, enables training models up to 4x larger than hardware memory while adapting to changes in operator sequences, improves performance by up to 38.94% compared to recomputation or high-degree parallelism."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to DAC 2026. Previously titled \"Chameleon: Taming Dynamic Operator Sequences for Memory-Intensive LLM Training.\""}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11076","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:54:12Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:54:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-16T02:33:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11443","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11443","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11443","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gaurab","familyName":"Chhetri","name":"Chhetri, Gaurab","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Darrell","familyName":"Anderson","name":"Anderson, Darrell","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boniphace","familyName":"Kutela","name":"Kutela, Boniphace","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Subasish","familyName":"Das","name":"Das, Subasish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Transformer-Based Cross-Platform Analysis of Public Discourse on the 15-Minute City Paradigm"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T21:36:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-29T00:08:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/icmla66185.2025.00062","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study presents the first multi-platform sentiment analysis of public opinion on the 15-minute city concept across Twitter, Reddit, and news media. Using compressed transformer models and Llama-3-8B for annotation, we classify sentiment across heterogeneous text domains. Our pipeline handles long-form and short-form text, supports consistent annotation, and enables reproducible evaluation. We benchmark five models (DistilRoBERTa, DistilBERT, MiniLM, ELECTRA, TinyBERT) using stratified 5-fold cross-validation, reporting F1-score, AUC, and training time. DistilRoBERTa achieved the highest F1 (0.8292), TinyBERT the best efficiency, and MiniLM the best cross-platform consistency. Results show News data yields inflated performance due to class imbalance, Reddit suffers from summarization loss, and Twitter offers moderate challenge. Compressed models perform competitively, challenging assumptions that larger models are necessary. We identify platform-specific trade-offs and propose directions for scalable, real-world sentiment classification in urban planning discourse."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This is the author's preprint version of a paper accepted for presentation at the 24th International Conference on Machine Learning and Applications (ICMLA 2025), December 3-5, 2025, Florida, USA. The final published version will appear in the official IEEE proceedings. Conference site: https://www.icmla-conference.org/icmla25/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11443","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-09-16T03:02:48Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:02:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-29T02:31:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11206","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11206","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11206","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tae Soo","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Tae Soo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heechan","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Heechan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yoonjoo","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Yoonjoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joseph","familyName":"Seering","name":"Seering, Joseph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juho","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Juho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evalet: Evaluating Large Language Models through Functional Fragmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T10:24:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:41:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T02:46:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:17:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T11:40:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-04T01:54:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-20T05:43:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-21T01:41:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Practitioners increasingly rely on Large Language Models (LLMs) to evaluate generative AI outputs through \"LLM-as-a-Judge\" approaches. However, these methods produce holistic scores that obscure which specific elements influenced the assessments. We propose functional fragmentation, a method that dissects each output into key fragments and interprets the rhetoric functions that each fragment serves relative to evaluation criteria -- surfacing the elements of interest and revealing how they fulfill or hinder user goals. We instantiate this approach in Evalet, an interactive system that visualizes fragment-level functions across many outputs to support inspection, rating, and comparison of evaluations. A user study (N=10) found that, while practitioners struggled to validate holistic scores, our approach helped them identify 48% more evaluation misalignments. This helped them calibrate trust in LLM evaluations and rely on them to find more actionable issues in model outputs. Our work shifts LLM evaluation from quantitative scores toward qualitative, fine-grained analysis of model behavior."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The first two authors hold equal contribution. Presented at CHI 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11206","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:57:13Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:57:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:48:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11452","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11452","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11452","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yining","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yining","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zilong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zilong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shiyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changlong","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Changlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingyu","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Qingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Zhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning to Optimize Multi-Objective Alignment Through Dynamic Reward Weighting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T21:56:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:54:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-30T18:06:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-01T00:02:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Prior work in multi-objective reinforcement learning typically uses linear reward scalarization with fixed weights, which provably fails to capture non-convex Pareto fronts and thus yields suboptimal results. This limitation becomes especially critical in online preference alignment for large language models. Here, stochastic trajectories generated by parameterized policies create highly non-linear and non-convex mappings from parameters to objectives that no single static weighting scheme can find optimal trade-offs. We address this limitation by introducing dynamic reward weighting, which adaptively adjusts reward weights during the online reinforcement learning process. Unlike existing approaches that rely on fixed-weight interpolation, our dynamic weighting continuously balances and prioritizes objectives in training, facilitating effective exploration of Pareto fronts in objective space. We introduce two approaches of increasing sophistication and generalizability: hypervolume-guided weight adaptation and gradient-based weight optimization, offering a versatile toolkit for online multi-objective alignment. Our extensive experiments demonstrate their compatibility with commonly used online reinforcement learning algorithms, effectiveness across multiple datasets, and applicability to different model families, consistently achieving Pareto dominant solutions with fewer training steps than fixed-weight linear scalarization baselines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11452","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:03:00Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:03:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-01T02:30:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11364","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11364","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11364","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Weiheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Heng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongpeng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Hongpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yusen","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Yusen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arash","familyName":"Ajoudani","name":"Ajoudani, Arash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yizhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ActivePose: Active 6D Object Pose Estimation and Tracking for Robotic Manipulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T17:39:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:50:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-07T13:25:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-10T00:44:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate 6-DoF object pose estimation and tracking are critical for reliable robotic manipulation. However, zero-shot methods often fail under viewpoint-induced ambiguities and fixed-camera setups struggle when objects move or become self-occluded. To address these challenges, we propose an active pose estimation pipeline that combines a Vision-Language Model (VLM) with \"robotic imagination\" to dynamically detect and resolve ambiguities in real time. In an offline stage, we render a dense set of views of the CAD model, compute the FoundationPose entropy for each view, and construct a geometric-aware prompt that includes low-entropy (unambiguous) and high-entropy (ambiguous) examples. At runtime, the system: (1) queries the VLM on the live image for an ambiguity score; (2) if ambiguity is detected, imagines a discrete set of candidate camera poses by rendering virtual views, scores each based on a weighted combination of VLM ambiguity probability and FoundationPose entropy, and then moves the camera to the Next-Best-View (NBV) to obtain a disambiguated pose estimation. Furthermore, since moving objects may leave the camera's field of view, we introduce an active pose tracking module: a diffusion-policy trained via imitation learning, which generates camera trajectories that preserve object visibility and minimize pose ambiguity. Experiments in simulation and real-world show that our approach significantly outperforms classical baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6D Pose, Diffusion Policy, Robot Learning"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11364","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:00:58Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:00:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-10T04:49:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10625","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10625","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10625","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iván Vicente Moreno","familyName":"Cencerrado","name":"Cencerrado, Iván Vicente Moreno","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arnau Padrés","familyName":"Masdemont","name":"Masdemont, Arnau Padrés","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anton Gonzalvez","familyName":"Hawthorne","name":"Hawthorne, Anton Gonzalvez","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David Demitri","familyName":"Africa","name":"Africa, David Demitri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Pacchiardi","name":"Pacchiardi, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"No Answer Needed: Predicting LLM Answer Accuracy from Question-Only Linear Probes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T18:09:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:04:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-09T12:15:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-10T02:48:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T10:11:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:41:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Do large language models (LLMs) anticipate when they will answer correctly? To study this, we extract activations after a question is read but before any tokens are generated, and train linear probes to predict whether the model's forthcoming answer will be correct. Across three open-source model families ranging from 7 to 70 billion parameters, projections on this \"in-advance correctness direction\" trained on generic trivia questions predict success in distribution and on diverse out-of-distribution knowledge datasets, indicating a deeper signal than dataset-specific spurious features, and outperforming black-box baselines and verbalised predicted confidence. Predictive power saturates in intermediate layers and, notably, generalisation falters on questions requiring mathematical reasoning. Moreover, for models responding \"I don't know\", doing so strongly correlates with the probe score, indicating that the same direction also captures confidence. By complementing previous results on truthfulness and other behaviours obtained with probes and sparse auto-encoders, our work contributes essential findings to elucidate LLM internals."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted (poster) to Principled Design for Trustworthy AI at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10625","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:43:38Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:43:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-04T03:17:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11079","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11079","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11079","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinwei","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Jinwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qizhen","familyName":"Lan","name":"Lan, Qizhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinghui","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Yinghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lifan","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Lifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiyou","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Weiyou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyuan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Xinyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lewei","familyName":"He","name":"He, Lewei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Jingsong","name":"Jingsong, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Difficulty-Aware Agentic Orchestration for Query-Specific Multi-Agent Workflows"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T03:57:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T02:48:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T13:32:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:52:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-11T12:33:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:35:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-08T06:37:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-09T02:14:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02-13T16:13:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-02-16T01:50:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Model (LLM)-based agentic systems have shown strong capabilities across various tasks. However, existing multi-agent frameworks often rely on static or task-level workflows, which either over-process simple queries or underperform on complex ones, while also neglecting the efficiency-performance trade-offs across heterogeneous LLMs. To address these limitations, we propose Difficulty-Aware Agentic Orchestration (DAAO), which can dynamically generate query-specific multi-agent workflows guided by predicted query difficulty. DAAO comprises three interdependent modules: a variational autoencoder (VAE) for difficulty estimation, a modular operator allocator, and a cost- and performance-aware LLM router. A self-adjusting policy updates difficulty estimates based on workflow success, enabling simpler workflows for easy queries and more complex strategies for harder ones. Experiments on six benchmarks demonstrate that DAAO surpasses prior multi-agent systems in both accuracy and inference efficiency, validating its effectiveness for adaptive, difficulty-aware reasoning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to WWW2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11079","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:54:16Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:54:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-16T02:59:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11362","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11362","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11362","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Loka","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Loka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wong Yu","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Wong Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minghao","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Minghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Guangyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenhao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gongxu","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Gongxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuewen","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Yuewen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Salman","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Salman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peter","familyName":"Spirtes","name":"Spirtes, Peter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PersonaX: Multimodal Datasets with LLM-Inferred Behavior Traits"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T17:30:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:49:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-10T12:33:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-11T01:48:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding human behavior traits is central to applications in human-computer interaction, computational social science, and personalized AI systems. Such understanding often requires integrating multiple modalities to capture nuanced patterns and relationships. However, existing resources rarely provide datasets that combine behavioral descriptors with complementary modalities such as facial attributes and biographical information. To address this gap, we present PersonaX, a curated collection of multimodal datasets designed to enable comprehensive analysis of public traits across modalities. PersonaX consists of (1) CelebPersona, featuring 9444 public figures from diverse occupations, and (2) AthlePersona, covering 4181 professional athletes across 7 major sports leagues. Each dataset includes behavioral trait assessments inferred by three high-performing large language models, alongside facial imagery and structured biographical features. We analyze PersonaX at two complementary levels. First, we abstract high-level trait scores from text descriptions and apply five statistical independence tests to examine their relationships with other modalities. Second, we introduce a novel causal representation learning (CRL) framework tailored to multimodal and multi-measurement data, providing theoretical identifiability guarantees. Experiments on both synthetic and real-world data demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach. By unifying structured and unstructured analysis, PersonaX establishes a foundation for studying LLM-inferred behavioral traits in conjunction with visual and biographical attributes, advancing multimodal trait analysis and causal reasoning. The code is available at https://github.com/lokali/PersonaX."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11362","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:00:55Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:00:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-11T03:28:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11361","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11361","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11361","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichen","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Yichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhang","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Yuhang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siteng","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Siteng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Guanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengpeng","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Zhengpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bojun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Bojun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujia","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yujia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Isaac N","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Isaac N","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lewei","familyName":"He","name":"He, Lewei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MAPGD: Multi-Agent Prompt Gradient Descent for Collaborative Prompt Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T17:28:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:49:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T09:25:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:35:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T09:31:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-04T01:46:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Prompt engineering is crucial for fully leveraging large language models (LLMs), yet most existing optimization methods follow a single trajectory, resulting in limited adaptability, gradient conflicts, and high computational overhead. We propose MAPGD (Multi-Agent Prompt Gradient Descent), a novel framework that reconceptualizes prompt optimization as a collaborative process among specialized agents. Each agent focuses on a distinct refinement dimension, such as instruction clarity, example selection, format structure, or stylistic adaptation, and their contributions are coordinated through semantic gradient embedding, conflict detection, and fusion. To further enhance robustness and stability, MAPGD introduces two new mechanisms: Hypersphere Constrained Gradient Clustering (HCGC), which enforces angular margin constraints for compact and well-separated clusters, and Channel Adaptive Agent Weighting (CAAW), which dynamically reweights agent contributions based on validation performance. Experiments on classification and reasoning benchmarks show that MAPGD consistently surpasses single-agent and random baselines in both accuracy and efficiency. Ablation studies confirm the effectiveness of gradient fusion, agent specialization, and conflict resolution. Together, these components establish MAPGD as a unified, gradient-based, and interpretable framework for robust prompt optimization with theoretical convergence guarantees."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11361","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:00:54Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:00:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-04T03:40:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11478","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11478","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11478","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew G.","familyName":"Breithaupt","name":"Breithaupt, Andrew G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nayoung","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Nayoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James D.","familyName":"Finch","name":"Finch, James D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeanne M.","familyName":"Powell","name":"Powell, Jeanne M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arin L.","familyName":"Nelson","name":"Nelson, Arin L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oz A.","familyName":"Alon","name":"Alon, Oz A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Howard J.","familyName":"Rosen","name":"Rosen, Howard J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinho D.","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Jinho D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Designing and Evaluating a Conversational Agent for Early Diagnosis of Alzheimer's Disease and Related Dementias"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T23:55:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:56:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-27T15:50:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-28T02:01:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Early diagnosis of Alzheimer's disease and related dementias (ADRD) is critical for timely intervention, yet most diagnoses are delayed until advanced stages. While comprehensive patient narratives are essential for accurate diagnosis, prior work has largely focused on screening studies that classify cognitive status from interactions rather than supporting the diagnostic process. We designed voice-interactive conversational agents, leveraging large language models (LLMs), to elicit narratives relevant to ADRD from patients and informants. We evaluated the agent with 30 adults with suspected ADRD through conversation analysis, user surveys, and analysis of symptom elicitation compared to blinded specialist interviews. Symptoms detected by the agent showed promising agreement with those identified by specialists. Users appreciated the agent's patience and systematic questioning, which supported engagement and expression of complex, hard-to-describe experiences. While these findings suggest potential for conversational agents as structured diagnostic support tools, further validation with larger samples and assessment of clinical utility is needed before deployment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"First two authors contributed equally"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11478","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:03:37Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:03:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-28T03:23:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11480","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11480","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11480","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amir","familyName":"Taherin","name":"Taherin, Amir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juyi","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Juyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arash","familyName":"Akbari","name":"Akbari, Arash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arman","familyName":"Akbari","name":"Akbari, Arman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Pu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiwei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Weiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Kaeli","name":"Kaeli, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanzhi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yanzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Cross-Platform Scaling of Vision-Language-Action Models from Edge to Cloud GPUs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Emerging Technologies (cs.ET)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T00:00:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:56:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-26T15:57:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-27T02:36:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models have emerged as powerful generalist policies for robotic control, yet their performance scaling across model architectures and hardware platforms, as well as their associated power budgets, remain poorly understood. This work presents an evaluation of five representative VLA models -- spanning state-of-the-art baselines and two newly proposed architectures -- targeting edge and datacenter GPU platforms. Using the LIBERO benchmark, we measure accuracy alongside system-level metrics, including latency, throughput, and peak memory usage, under varying edge power constraints and high-performance datacenter GPU configurations. Our results identify distinct scaling trends: (1) architectural choices, such as action tokenization and model backbone size, strongly influence throughput and memory footprint; (2) power-constrained edge devices exhibit non-linear performance degradation, with some configurations matching or exceeding older datacenter GPUs; and (3) high-throughput variants can be achieved without significant accuracy loss. These findings provide actionable insights when selecting and optimizing VLAs across a range of deployment constraints. Our work challenges current assumptions about the superiority of datacenter hardware for robotic inference."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear in the Asilomar Conference on Signals, Systems, and Computers 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11480","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:03:40Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:03:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:34:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10769","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10769","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10769","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tara","familyName":"Bogavelli","name":"Bogavelli, Tara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roshnee","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Roshnee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hari","familyName":"Subramani","name":"Subramani, Hari","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgentArch: A Comprehensive Benchmark to Evaluate Agent Architectures in Enterprise"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T01:18:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:13:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-06T18:18:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-07T01:56:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While individual components of agentic architectures have been studied in isolation, there remains limited empirical understanding of how different design dimensions interact within complex multi-agent systems. This study aims to address these gaps by providing a comprehensive enterprise-specific benchmark evaluating 18 distinct agentic configurations across state-of-the-art large language models. We examine four critical agentic system dimensions: orchestration strategy, agent prompt implementation (ReAct versus function calling), memory architecture, and thinking tool integration. Our benchmark reveals significant model-specific architectural preferences that challenge the prevalent one-size-fits-all paradigm in agentic AI systems. It also reveals significant weaknesses in overall agentic performance on enterprise tasks with the highest scoring models achieving a maximum of only 35.3\\% success on the more complex task and 70.8\\% on the simpler task. We hope these findings inform the design of future agentic systems by enabling more empirically backed decisions regarding architectural components and model selection."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10769","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:47:01Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:47:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-07T02:59:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10918","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10918","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10918","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbo","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Wenbo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaoyi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Shaoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengjie","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shengjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ToMA: Token Merge with Attention for Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T17:35:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:23:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-23T02:10:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:22:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-30T17:46:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-02T01:53:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models excel in high-fidelity image generation but face scalability limits due to transformers' quadratic attention complexity. Plug-and-play token reduction methods like ToMeSD and ToFu reduce FLOPs by merging redundant tokens in generated images but rely on GPU-inefficient operations (e.g., sorting, scattered writes), introducing overheads that negate theoretical speedups when paired with optimized attention implementations (e.g., FlashAttention). To bridge this gap, we propose Token Merge with Attention (ToMA), an off-the-shelf method that redesigns token reduction for GPU-aligned efficiency, with three key contributions: 1) a reformulation of token merge as a submodular optimization problem to select diverse tokens; 2) merge/unmerge as an attention-like linear transformation via GPU-friendly matrix operations; and 3) exploiting latent locality and sequential redundancy (pattern reuse) to minimize overhead. ToMA reduces SDXL/Flux generation latency by 24%/23%, respectively (with DINO $Δ\u0026lt; 0.07$), outperforming prior methods. This work bridges the gap between theoretical and practical efficiency for transformers in diffusion. Code available at https://github.com/WenboLuu/ToMA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"In proceedings of the 42nd International Conference on Machine Learning (ICML 2025). Code available at https://github.com/wenboluu/ToMA"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10918","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:50:31Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:50:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-02T04:46:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10641","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10641","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10641","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikita","familyName":"Rajaneesh","name":"Rajaneesh, Nikita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Zollo","name":"Zollo, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard","familyName":"Zemel","name":"Zemel, Richard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Test-Time Warmup for Multimodal Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T18:58:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:05:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-06T12:24:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-07T01:39:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) hold great promise for advanced reasoning at the intersection of text and images, yet they have not fully realized this potential. MLLMs typically integrate an LLM, a vision encoder, and a connector that maps the vision encoder's embeddings into the LLM's text embedding space. Although each component is pretrained on massive datasets with billions of samples, the entire multimodal model is typically trained on only thousands (or a few million) samples, which can result in weak performance on complex reasoning tasks. To address these shortcomings, instead of relying on extensive labeled datasets for fine-tuning, we propose a Test-Time Warmup method that adapts the MLLM per test instance by leveraging data from weakly supervised auxiliary tasks. With our approach, we observe a relative performance improvement of 4.03% on MMMU, 5.28% on VQA-Rad, and 1.63% on GQA on the Llama-Vision-Instruct model. Our method demonstrates that 'warming up' before inference can enhance MLLMs' robustness across diverse reasoning tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10641","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:44:00Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:44:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-07T03:05:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10687","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10687","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10687","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chun-Han","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Chun-Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Donné","name":"Donné, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Narendra","familyName":"Ahuja","name":"Ahuja, Narendra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Varun","familyName":"Jampani","name":"Jampani, Varun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Stable Part Diffusion 4D: Multi-View RGB and Kinematic Parts Video Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T20:39:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:08:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-04T23:02:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-06T01:11:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Stable Part Diffusion 4D (SP4D), a framework for generating paired RGB and kinematic part videos from monocular inputs. Unlike conventional part segmentation methods that rely on appearance-based semantic cues, SP4D learns to produce kinematic parts - structural components aligned with object articulation and consistent across views and time. SP4D adopts a dual-branch diffusion model that jointly synthesizes RGB frames and corresponding part segmentation maps. To simplify the architecture and flexibly enable different part counts, we introduce a spatial color encoding scheme that maps part masks to continuous RGB-like images. This encoding allows the segmentation branch to share the latent VAE from the RGB branch, while enabling part segmentation to be recovered via straightforward post-processing. A Bidirectional Diffusion Fusion (BiDiFuse) module enhances cross-branch consistency, supported by a contrastive part consistency loss to promote spatial and temporal alignment of part predictions. We demonstrate that the generated 2D part maps can be lifted to 3D to derive skeletal structures and harmonic skinning weights with few manual adjustments. To train and evaluate SP4D, we construct KinematicParts20K, a curated dataset of over 20K rigged objects selected and processed from Objaverse XL (Deitke et al., 2023), each paired with multi-view RGB and part video sequences. Experiments show that SP4D generalizes strongly to diverse scenarios, including real-world videos, novel generated objects, and rare articulated poses, producing kinematic-aware outputs suitable for downstream animation and motion-related tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Page: https://stablepartdiffusion4d.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10687","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:45:04Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:45:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-06T03:15:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11348","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11348","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11348","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Viet-Hoang","familyName":"Tran","name":"Tran, Viet-Hoang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Van Hoan","familyName":"Trinh","name":"Trinh, Van Hoan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Khanh Vinh","familyName":"Bui","name":"Bui, Khanh Vinh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tan M.","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Tan M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On Linear Mode Connectivity of Mixture-of-Experts Architectures"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T16:51:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:49:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-25T03:12:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:23:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Linear Mode Connectivity (LMC) is a notable phenomenon in the loss landscapes of neural networks, wherein independently trained models have been observed to be connected--up to permutation symmetries--by linear paths in parameter space along which the loss remains consistently low. This observation challenges classical views of non-convex optimization and has implications for model ensembling, generalization, and our understanding of neural loss geometry. Inspired by recent studies on LMC in standard neural networks, we systematically investigate this phenomenon within Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architectures--a class of models known for their scalability and computational efficiency, which combine traditional neural networks--referred to as experts--through a learnable gating mechanism. We begin by conducting a comprehensive analysis of both dense and sparse gating regimes, demonstrating that the symmetries inherent to MoE architectures are fully characterized by permutations acting on both the expert components and the gating function. Building on these foundational findings, we propose a matching algorithm that enables alignment between independently trained MoEs, thereby facilitating the discovery of LMC. Finally, we empirically validate the presence of LMC using our proposed algorithm across diverse MoE configurations--including dense, sparse, and shared-expert variants--under a wide range of model settings and datasets of varying scales and modalities. Our results confirm the existence of LMC in MoE architectures and offer fundamental insights into the functional landscape and optimization dynamics of deep learning models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11348","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:00:36Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:00:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T04:02:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11465","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11465","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11465","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amirhossein","familyName":"Abaskohi","name":"Abaskohi, Amirhossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raymond","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Raymond","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuyuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chuyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shafiq","familyName":"Joty","name":"Joty, Shafiq","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giuseppe","familyName":"Carenini","name":"Carenini, Giuseppe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CEMTM: Contextual Embedding-based Multimodal Topic Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T23:07:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:55:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-05T16:11:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:00:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce CEMTM, a context-enhanced multimodal topic model designed to infer coherent and interpretable topic structures from both short and long documents containing text and images. CEMTM builds on fine-tuned large vision language models (LVLMs) to obtain contextualized embeddings, and employs a distributional attention mechanism to weight token-level contributions to topic inference. A reconstruction objective aligns topic-based representations with the document embedding, encouraging semantic consistency across modalities. Unlike existing approaches, CEMTM can process multiple images per document without repeated encoding and maintains interpretability through explicit word-topic and document-topic distributions. Extensive experiments on six multimodal benchmarks show that CEMTM consistently outperforms unimodal and multimodal baselines, achieving a remarkable average LLM score of 2.61. Further analysis shows its effectiveness in downstream few-shot retrieval and its ability to capture visually grounded semantics in complex domains such as scientific articles."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11465","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:03:19Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:03:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:49:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11425","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11425","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11425","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Mubtasim","familyName":"Ahasan","name":"Ahasan, Md Mubtasim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rafat Hasan","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Rafat Hasan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tasnim","familyName":"Mohiuddin","name":"Mohiuddin, Tasnim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aman","familyName":"Chadha","name":"Chadha, Aman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tariq","familyName":"Iqbal","name":"Iqbal, Tariq","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M Ashraful","familyName":"Amin","name":"Amin, M Ashraful","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amin Ahsan","familyName":"Ali","name":"Ali, Amin Ahsan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Mofijul","familyName":"Islam","name":"Islam, Md Mofijul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A K M Mahbubur","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, A K M Mahbubur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FuseCodec: Semantic-Contextual Fusion and Supervision for Neural Codecs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T20:35:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:52:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-29T08:43:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-30T01:45:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Speech tokenization enables discrete representation and facilitates speech language modeling. However, existing neural codecs capture low-level acoustic features, overlooking the semantic and contextual cues inherent to human speech. While recent efforts introduced semantic representations from self-supervised speech models or incorporated contextual representations from pre-trained language models, challenges remain in aligning and unifying the semantic and contextual representations. We introduce FuseCodec, which unifies acoustic, semantic, and contextual representations through strong cross-modal alignment and globally informed supervision. We propose three complementary techniques: (i) Latent Representation Fusion, integrating semantic and contextual features directly into the encoder latent space for robust and unified representation learning; (ii) Global Semantic-Contextual Supervision, supervising discrete tokens with globally pooled and broadcasted representations to enhance temporal consistency and cross-modal alignment; and (iii) Temporally Aligned Contextual Supervision, strengthening alignment by dynamically matching contextual and speech tokens within a local window for fine-grained token-level supervision. We further introduce FuseCodec-TTS, demonstrating our methodology's applicability to zero-shot speech synthesis. Empirically, FuseCodec achieves state-of-the-art performance in LibriSpeech, surpassing EnCodec, SpeechTokenizer, and DAC in transcription accuracy, perceptual quality, intelligibility, and speaker similarity. Results highlight the effectiveness of contextually and semantically guided tokenization for speech tokenization and downstream tasks. Code and pretrained models are available at https://github.com/mubtasimahasan/FuseCodec."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11425","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:02:23Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:02:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-30T05:19:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10739","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10739","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10739","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mobina","familyName":"Pournemat","name":"Pournemat, Mobina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keivan","familyName":"Rezaei","name":"Rezaei, Keivan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gaurang","familyName":"Sriramanan","name":"Sriramanan, Gaurang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arman","familyName":"Zarei","name":"Zarei, Arman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxiang","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Jiaxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hamid","familyName":"Eghbalzadeh","name":"Eghbalzadeh, Hamid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soheil","familyName":"Feizi","name":"Feizi, Soheil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reasoning Under Uncertainty: Exploring Probabilistic Reasoning Capabilities of LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T22:58:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:11:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-26T15:28:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-29T01:10:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite widespread success in language understanding and generation, large language models (LLMs) exhibit unclear and often inconsistent behavior when faced with tasks that require probabilistic reasoning. In this work, we present the first comprehensive study of the reasoning capabilities of LLMs over explicit discrete probability distributions. Given observations from a probability distribution, we evaluate models on three carefully designed tasks, mode identification, maximum likelihood estimation, and sample generation, by prompting them to provide responses to queries about either the joint distribution or its conditionals. These tasks thus probe a range of probabilistic skills, including frequency analysis, marginalization, and generative behavior. Through comprehensive empirical evaluations, we demonstrate that there exists a clear performance gap between smaller and larger models, with the latter demonstrating stronger inference and surprising capabilities in sample generation. Furthermore, our investigations reveal notable limitations, including sensitivity to variations in the notation utilized to represent probabilistic outcomes and performance degradation of over 60% as context length increases. Together, our results provide a detailed understanding of the probabilistic reasoning abilities of LLMs and identify key directions for future improvement."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10739","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:46:18Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:46:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-29T02:56:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10655","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10655","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10655","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charankumar","familyName":"Akiri","name":"Akiri, Charankumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harrison","familyName":"Simpson","name":"Simpson, Harrison","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kshitiz","familyName":"Aryal","name":"Aryal, Kshitiz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aarav","familyName":"Khanna","name":"Khanna, Aarav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maanak","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Maanak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Safety and Security Analysis of Large Language Models: Benchmarking Risk Profile and Harm Potential"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T19:34:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:06:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-26T02:00:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-29T00:22:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While the widespread deployment of Large Language Models (LLMs) holds great potential for society, their vulnerabilities to adversarial manipulation and exploitation can pose serious safety, security, and ethical risks. As new threats continue to emerge, it becomes critically necessary to assess the landscape of LLMs' safety and security against evolving adversarial prompt techniques. To understand the behavior of LLMs, this research provides an empirical analysis and risk profile of nine prominent LLMs, Claude Opus 4, DeepSeek V3 (both open-source and online), Gemini 2.5 Flash, GPT-4o, Grok 3, Llama 4 Scout, Mistral 7B, and Qwen 3 1.7B, against 24 different security and safety categories. These LLMs are evaluated on their ability to produce harmful responses for adversarially crafted prompts (dataset has been made public) for a broad range of safety and security topics, such as promotion of violent criminal behavior, promotion of non-violent criminal activity, societal harms related to safety, illegal sexual content, dangerous code generation, and cybersecurity threats beyond code. Our study introduces the Risk Severity Index (RSI), an agile and scalable evaluation score, to quantify and compare the security posture and creating a risk profile of LLMs. As the LLM development landscape progresses, the RSI is intended to be a valuable metric for comparing the risks of LLMs across evolving threats. This research finds widespread vulnerabilities in the safety filters of the LLMs tested and highlights the urgent need for stronger alignment, responsible deployment practices, and model governance, particularly for open-access and rapidly iterated models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10655","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:44:20Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:44:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-29T02:52:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11101","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11101","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11101","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haokun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Haokun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yazhou","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yazhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jizhi","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Jizhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiuchi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qiuchi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Seeing is Not Understanding: A Benchmark on Perception-Cognition Disparities in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T05:40:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:34:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-22T08:45:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:20:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T02:12:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:22:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the rapid advancement of Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs), they have demonstrated exceptional capabilities across a variety of vision-language tasks. However, current evaluation benchmarks predominantly focus on objective visual question answering or captioning, inadequately assessing the models' ability to understand complex and subjective human emotions. To bridge this gap, we introduce EmoBench-Reddit, a novel, hierarchical benchmark for multimodal emotion understanding. The dataset comprises 350 meticulously curated samples from the social media platform Reddit, each containing an image, associated user-provided text, and an emotion category (sad, humor, sarcasm, happy) confirmed by user flairs. We designed a hierarchical task framework that progresses from basic perception to advanced cognition, with each data point featuring six multiple-choice questions and one open-ended question of increasing difficulty. Perception tasks evaluate the model's ability to identify basic visual elements (e.g., colors, objects), while cognition tasks require scene reasoning, intent understanding, and deep empathy integrating textual context. We ensured annotation quality through a combination of AI assistance (Claude 4) and manual verification.We conducted a comprehensive evaluation of nine leading MLLMs, including GPT-5, Gemini-2.5-pro, and GPT-4o, on EmoBench-Reddit."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"I need to modify the content of the article"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11101","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:54:46Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:54:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-24T02:35:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11417","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11417","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11417","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shresth","familyName":"Grover","name":"Grover, Shresth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akshay","familyName":"Gopalkrishnan","name":"Gopalkrishnan, Akshay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Ai","name":"Ai, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henrik I.","familyName":"Christensen","name":"Christensen, Henrik I.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanlin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xuanlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing Generalization in Vision-Language-Action Models by Preserving Pretrained Representations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T20:08:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:52:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T02:41:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:18:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language-action (VLA) models finetuned from vision-language models (VLMs) hold the promise of leveraging rich pretrained representations to build generalist robots across diverse tasks and environments. However, direct fine-tuning on robot data often disrupts these representations and limits generalization. We present a framework that better preserves pretrained features while adapting them for robot manipulation. Our approach introduces three components: (i) a dual-encoder design with one frozen vision encoder to retain pretrained features and another trainable for task adaptation, (ii) a string-based action tokenizer that casts continuous actions into character sequences aligned with the model's pretraining domain, and (iii) a co-training strategy that combines robot demonstrations with vision-language datasets emphasizing spatial reasoning and affordances. Evaluations in simulation and on real robots show that our method improves robustness to visual perturbations, generalization to novel instructions and environments, and overall task success compared to baselines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project Page: https://gen-vla.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11417","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:02:12Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:02:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-18T17:44:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10970","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10970","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10970","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joshua Au","familyName":"Yeung","name":"Yeung, Joshua Au","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacopo","familyName":"Dalmasso","name":"Dalmasso, Jacopo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Foschini","name":"Foschini, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Richard JB","familyName":"Dobson","name":"Dobson, Richard JB","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeljko","familyName":"Kraljevic","name":"Kraljevic, Zeljko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Psychogenic Machine: Simulating AI Psychosis, Delusion Reinforcement and Harm Enablement in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T20:10:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:26:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-17T01:44:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-18T00:15:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Background: Emerging reports of \"AI psychosis\" are on the rise, where user-LLM interactions may exacerbate or induce psychosis or adverse psychological symptoms. Whilst the sycophantic and agreeable nature of LLMs can be beneficial, it becomes a vector for harm by reinforcing delusional beliefs in vulnerable users.\n Methods: Psychosis-bench is a novel benchmark designed to systematically evaluate the psychogenicity of LLMs comprises 16 structured, 12-turn conversational scenarios simulating the progression of delusional themes(Erotic Delusions, Grandiose/Messianic Delusions, Referential Delusions) and potential harms. We evaluated eight prominent LLMs for Delusion Confirmation (DCS), Harm Enablement (HES), and Safety Intervention(SIS) across explicit and implicit conversational contexts.\n Findings: Across 1,536 simulated conversation turns, all LLMs demonstrated psychogenic potential, showing a strong tendency to perpetuate rather than challenge delusions (mean DCS of 0.91 $\\pm$0.88). Models frequently enabled harmful user requests (mean HES of 0.69 $\\pm$0.84) and offered safety interventions in only roughly a third of applicable turns (mean SIS of 0.37 $\\pm$0.48). 51 / 128 (39.8%) of scenarios had no safety interventions offered. Performance was significantly worse in implicit scenarios, models were more likely to confirm delusions and enable harm while offering fewer interventions (p \u0026lt; .001). A strong correlation was found between DCS and HES (rs = .77). Model performance varied widely, indicating that safety is not an emergent property of scale alone.\n Conclusion: This study establishes LLM psychogenicity as a quantifiable risk and underscores the urgent need for re-thinking how we train LLMs. We frame this issue not merely as a technical challenge but as a public health imperative requiring collaboration between developers, policymakers, and healthcare professionals."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10970","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:51:43Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:51:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-18T17:43:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11177","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11177","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11177","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yawei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luca","familyName":"Benini","name":"Benini, Luca","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Optimal Brain Restoration for Joint Quantization and Sparsification of LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T09:17:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:39:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T03:17:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:18:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in Large Language Model (LLM) compression, such as quantization and pruning, have achieved notable success. However, as these techniques gradually approach their respective limits, relying on a single method for further compression has become increasingly challenging. In this work, we explore an alternative solution by combining quantization and sparsity. This joint approach, though promising, introduces new difficulties due to the inherently conflicting requirements on weight distributions: quantization favors compact ranges, while pruning benefits from high variance. To attack this problem, we propose Optimal Brain Restoration (OBR), a general and training-free framework that aligns pruning and quantization by error compensation between both. OBR minimizes performance degradation on downstream tasks by building on a second-order Hessian objective, which is then reformulated into a tractable problem through surrogate approximation and ultimately reaches a closed-form solution via group error compensation. Experiments show that OBR enables aggressive W4A4KV4 quantization with 50% sparsity on existing LLMs, and delivers up to 4.72x speedup and 6.4x memory reduction compared to the FP16-dense baseline."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11177","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:56:33Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:56:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T02:12:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11067","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11067","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11067","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangxuan","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Liangxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingqian","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Qingqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kangning","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Kangning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xun","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Xun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianzhe","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Xianzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangfu","familyName":"Hao","name":"Hao, Guangfu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agentic Lybic: Multi-Agent Execution System with Tiered Reasoning and Orchestration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T03:22:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T01:16:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T02:49:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-17T00:17:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autonomous agents for desktop automation struggle with complex multi-step tasks due to poor coordination and inadequate quality control. We introduce Agentic Lybic, a novel multi-agent system where the entire architecture operates as a finite-state machine (FSM). This core innovation enables dynamic orchestration. Our system comprises four components: a Controller, a Manager, three Workers (Technician for code-based operations, Operator for GUI interactions, and Analyst for decision support), and an Evaluator. The critical mechanism is the FSM-based routing between these components, which provides flexibility and generalization by dynamically selecting the optimal execution strategy for each subtask. This principled orchestration, combined with robust quality gating, enables adaptive replanning and error recovery. Evaluated officially on the OSWorld benchmark, Agentic Lybic achieves a state-of-the-art 57.07% success rate in 50 steps, substantially outperforming existing methods. Results demonstrate that principled multi-agent orchestration with continuous quality control provides superior reliability for generalized desktop automation in complex computing environments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11067","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:53:59Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:54:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-17T02:12:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11511","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11511","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11511","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suman","familyName":"Cha","name":"Cha, Suman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunjoong","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hyunjoong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning Majority-to-Minority Transformations with MMD and Triplet Loss for Imbalanced Classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T01:47:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:57:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Class imbalance in supervised classification often degrades model performance by biasing predictions toward the majority class, particularly in critical applications such as medical diagnosis and fraud detection. Traditional oversampling techniques, including SMOTE and its variants, generate synthetic minority samples via local interpolation but fail to capture global data distributions in high-dimensional spaces. Deep generative models based on GANs offer richer distribution modeling yet suffer from training instability and mode collapse under severe imbalance. To overcome these limitations, we introduce an oversampling framework that learns a parametric transformation to map majority samples into the minority distribution. Our approach minimizes the maximum mean discrepancy (MMD) between transformed and true minority samples for global alignment, and incorporates a triplet loss regularizer to enforce boundary awareness by guiding synthesized samples toward challenging borderline regions. We evaluate our method on 29 synthetic and real-world datasets, demonstrating consistent improvements over classical and generative baselines in AUROC, G-mean, F1-score, and MCC. These results confirm the robustness, computational efficiency, and practical utility of the proposed framework for imbalanced classification tasks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":".19 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11511","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:04:24Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:04:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:04:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11492","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11492","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11492","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anirban Saha","familyName":"Anik","name":"Anik, Anirban Saha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Fahimul Kabir","familyName":"Chowdhury","name":"Chowdhury, Md Fahimul Kabir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Wyckoff","name":"Wyckoff, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sagnik Ray","familyName":"Choudhury","name":"Choudhury, Sagnik Ray","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ClaimIQ at CheckThat! 2025: Comparing Prompted and Fine-Tuned Language Models for Verifying Numerical Claims"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-15T01:03:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:57:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents our system for Task 3 of the CLEF 2025 CheckThat! Lab, which focuses on verifying numerical and temporal claims using retrieved evidence. We explore two complementary approaches: zero-shot prompting with instruction-tuned large language models (LLMs) and supervised fine-tuning using parameter-efficient LoRA. To enhance evidence quality, we investigate several selection strategies, including full-document input and top-k sentence filtering using BM25 and MiniLM. Our best-performing model LLaMA fine-tuned with LoRA achieves strong performance on the English validation set. However, a notable drop in the test set highlights a generalization challenge. These findings underscore the importance of evidence granularity and model adaptation for robust numerical fact verification."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Notebook for the CheckThat! Lab at CLEF 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11492","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:03:57Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:03:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:03:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11431","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11431","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11431","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aadil Gani","familyName":"Ganie","name":"Ganie, Aadil Gani","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Securing AI Agents: Implementing Role-Based Access Control for Industrial Applications"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T20:58:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:53:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The emergence of Large Language Models (LLMs) has significantly advanced solutions across various domains, from political science to software development. However, these models are constrained by their training data, which is static and limited to information available up to a specific date. Additionally, their generalized nature often necessitates fine-tuning -- whether for classification or instructional purposes -- to effectively perform specific downstream tasks. AI agents, leveraging LLMs as their core, mitigate some of these limitations by accessing external tools and real-time data, enabling applications such as live weather reporting and data analysis. In industrial settings, AI agents are transforming operations by enhancing decision-making, predictive maintenance, and process optimization. For example, in manufacturing, AI agents enable near-autonomous systems that boost productivity and support real-time decision-making. Despite these advancements, AI agents remain vulnerable to security threats, including prompt injection attacks, which pose significant risks to their integrity and reliability. To address these challenges, this paper proposes a framework for integrating Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) into AI agents, providing a robust security guardrail. This framework aims to support the effective and scalable deployment of AI agents, with a focus on on-premises implementations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11431","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:02:31Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:02:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:02:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11414","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11414","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11414","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abraham Toluwase","familyName":"Owodunni","name":"Owodunni, Abraham Toluwase","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sachin","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Sachin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Continually Adding New Languages to Multilingual Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T20:08:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:52:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multilingual language models are trained on a fixed set of languages, and to support new languages, the models need to be retrained from scratch. This is an expensive endeavor and is often infeasible, as model developers tend not to release their pre-training data. Naive approaches, such as continued pretraining, suffer from catastrophic forgetting; however, mitigation strategies like experience replay cannot be applied due to the lack of original pretraining data. In this work, we investigate the problem of continually adding new languages to a multilingual model, assuming access to pretraining data in only the target languages. We explore multiple approaches to address this problem and propose Layer-Selective LoRA (LayRA), which adds Low-Rank Adapters (LoRA) to selected initial and final layers while keeping the rest of the model frozen. LayRA builds on two insights: (1) LoRA reduces forgetting, and (2) multilingual models encode inputs in the source language in the initial layers, reason in English in intermediate layers, and translate back to the source language in final layers. We experiment with adding multiple combinations of Galician, Swahili, and Urdu to pretrained language models and evaluate each method on diverse multilingual tasks. We find that LayRA provides the overall best tradeoff between preserving models' capabilities in previously supported languages, while being competitive with existing approaches such as LoRA in learning new languages. We also demonstrate that using model arithmetic, the adapted models can be equipped with strong instruction following abilities without access to any instruction tuning data in the target languages."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11414","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:02:07Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:02:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:02:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11367","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11367","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11367","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chang-Hwan","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Chang-Hwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Shim","name":"Shim, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Detecting Model Drifts in Non-Stationary Environment Using Edit Operation Measures"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T17:48:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:50:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) agents typically assume stationary environment dynamics. Yet in real-world applications such as healthcare, robotics, and finance, transition probabilities or reward functions may evolve, leading to model drift. This paper proposes a novel framework to detect such drifts by analyzing the distributional changes in sequences of agent behavior. Specifically, we introduce a suite of edit operation-based measures to quantify deviations between state-action trajectories generated under stationary and perturbed conditions. Our experiments demonstrate that these measures can effectively distinguish drifted from non-drifted scenarios, even under varying levels of noise, providing a practical tool for drift detection in non-stationary RL environments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"28 pages, 3 figures, 17 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11367","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T03:01:02Z","registered":"2025-09-16T03:01:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T03:01:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11287","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11287","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11287","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ziqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunfeng","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Chunfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Congxuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Congxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaojuan","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Xiaojuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Bing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiming","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Weiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mitigating Hallucinations in Large Vision-Language Models by Self-Injecting Hallucinations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T14:26:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:45:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) suffer from serious hallucination problems, where the model-generated responses are inconsistent with the visual inputs. Existing hallucination mitigation methods are mainly based on preference alignment and require external human annotations or auxiliary models for preference data collection, which increase costs and limit sustainable improvement. To tackle these challenges, we propose Autonomous Preference Alignment via Self-Injection (APASI), a novel and generalizable method that mitigates hallucinations without external dependencies. APASI leverages the target LVLM to self-inject hallucinations into a generated response, creating a pair of responses with varying preference levels. During the self-injection process, the dis-preferred response is generated based on three key observations of hallucinations, ensuring it simulates real hallucination patterns. This fidelity offers an accurate learning signal for hallucination mitigation. Moreover, APASI incorporates an iterative alignment training strategy combined with curriculum learning to periodically update the preference data with increasing challenge, enabling stable and continuous enhancement of the LVLM. Extensive experiments across six benchmarks show that APASI not only effectively mitigates hallucinations for three baseline models but also achieves comparable or even superior performance to alignment-based methods with external dependency, thereby demonstrating its effectiveness and generalization capability. The code is available at https://github.com/davidluciolu/APASI."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"emnlp 2025 accepted"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11287","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:59:07Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:59:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:59:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11155","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11155","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11155","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Santhosh G","familyName":"S","name":"S, Santhosh G","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saurav","familyName":"Prakash","name":"Prakash, Saurav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Balaraman","familyName":"Ravindran","name":"Ravindran, Balaraman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AQUA: Attention via QUery mAgnitudes for Memory and Compute Efficient Inference in LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T08:20:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:38:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The quadratic complexity of the attention mechanism remains a fundamental barrier to scaling Large Language Models (LLMs) to longer contexts, creating a critical bottleneck in both computation and memory. To address this, we introduce AQUA (Attention via QUery mAgnitudes) a novel and versatile approximation strategy that significantly reduces the cost of attention with a graceful performance trade-off. Our method operates in two phases: an efficient offline step where we compute a universal, language agnostic projection matrix via SVD on a calibration dataset, and an online inference step where we project query and key vectors and dynamically select a sparse subset of dimensions based on the query's magnitude. We provide a formal theoretical analysis of AQUA, establishing the break-even point at which it becomes more computationally efficient than standard attention. Our empirical evaluations on state-of-the-art models like Llama-3.1-8B demonstrate that a 25% reduction in the attention dot-product computation can be achieved with a statistically insignificant impact on performance across a wide range of benchmarks. We further showcase the versatility of AQUA by demonstrating its ability to synergistically accelerate existing token eviction methods like H2O and to directly reduce KV-cache memory size. By offering a controllable knob to balance efficiency and accuracy, AQUA provides a practical and powerful tool for making large-scale LLM inference more accessible and sustainable."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11155","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:56:02Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:56:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:56:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11134","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11134","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11134","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaang","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Jiaang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shenglin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Shenglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shiyou","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Shiyou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dingyu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Dingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kangjin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Kangjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenzhi","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Chenzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinghao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Yinghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qin","familyName":"Hua","name":"Hua, Qin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanwen","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Hanwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenchao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Wenchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongqing","familyName":"Bao","name":"Bao, Dongqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyu","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Tianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangtao","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Guangtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guodong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Guodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liping","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Liping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GFS: A Preemption-aware Scheduling Framework for GPU Clusters with Predictive Spot Instance Management"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T07:05:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:36:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The surge in large language models (LLMs) has fundamentally reshaped the landscape of GPU usage patterns, creating an urgent need for more efficient management strategies. While cloud providers employ spot instances to reduce costs for low-priority (LP) tasks, existing schedulers still grapple with high eviction rates and lengthy queuing times. To address these limitations, we present GFS, a novel preemptive scheduling framework that enhances service-level objective (SLO) compliance for high-priority (HP) tasks while minimizing preemptions to LP tasks. Firstly, GFS utilizes a lightweight forecasting model that predicts GPU demand among different tenants, enabling proactive resource management. Secondly, GFS employs a dynamic allocation mechanism to adjust the spot quota for LP tasks with guaranteed durations. Lastly, GFS incorporates a preemptive scheduling policy that prioritizes HP tasks while minimizing the impact on LP tasks. We demonstrate the effectiveness of GFS through both real-world implementation and simulations. The results show that GFS reduces eviction rates by 33.0\\%, and cuts queuing delays by 44.1\\% for LP tasks. Furthermore, GFS enhances the GPU allocation rate by up to 22.8\\% in real production clusters. In a production cluster of more than 10,000 GPUs, GFS yields roughly \\$459,715 in monthly benefits."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper has been accepted to the 31st ACM International Conference on Architectural Support for Programming Languages and Operating Systems (ASPLOS 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11134","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:55:32Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:55:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:55:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11106","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11106","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11106","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Valentin","familyName":"Hofmann","name":"Hofmann, Valentin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Heineman","name":"Heineman, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ian","familyName":"Magnusson","name":"Magnusson, Ian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyle","familyName":"Lo","name":"Lo, Kyle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jesse","familyName":"Dodge","name":"Dodge, Jesse","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maarten","familyName":"Sap","name":"Sap, Maarten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pang Wei","familyName":"Koh","name":"Koh, Pang Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hannaneh","familyName":"Hajishirzi","name":"Hajishirzi, Hannaneh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Noah A.","familyName":"Smith","name":"Smith, Noah A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fluid Language Model Benchmarking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T05:49:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:34:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Language model (LM) benchmarking faces several challenges: comprehensive evaluations are costly, benchmarks often fail to measure the intended capabilities, and evaluation quality can degrade due to labeling errors and benchmark saturation. Although various strategies have been proposed to mitigate these issues, they tend to address individual aspects in isolation, neglecting broader questions about overall evaluation quality. Here, we introduce Fluid Benchmarking, a new evaluation approach that advances LM benchmarking across multiple dimensions. Inspired by psychometrics, Fluid Benchmarking is based on the insight that the relative value of benchmark items depends on an LM's capability level, suggesting that evaluation should adapt to each LM. Methodologically, Fluid Benchmarking estimates an item response model based on existing LM evaluation results and uses the inferred quantities to select evaluation items dynamically, similar to computerized adaptive testing in education. In our experiments, we compare Fluid Benchmarking against the common practice of random item sampling as well as more sophisticated baselines, including alternative methods grounded in item response theory. We examine four dimensions -- efficiency, validity, variance, and saturation -- and find that Fluid Benchmarking achieves superior performance in all of them (e.g., higher validity and less variance on MMLU with fifty times fewer items). Our analysis shows that the two components of Fluid Benchmarking have distinct effects: item response theory, used to map performance into a latent ability space, increases validity, while dynamic item selection reduces variance. Overall, our results suggest that LM benchmarking can be substantially improved by moving beyond static evaluation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"COLM 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11106","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":3,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:54:53Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:54:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:54:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11068","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11068","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11068","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zan-Kai","familyName":"Chong","name":"Chong, Zan-Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hiroyuki","familyName":"Ohsaki","name":"Ohsaki, Hiroyuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bryan","familyName":"Ng","name":"Ng, Bryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Tractable Asymmetric Verification for Large Language Models via Deterministic Replicability"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T03:30:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:32:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The landscape of Large Language Models (LLMs) shifts rapidly towards dynamic, multi-agent systems. This introduces a fundamental challenge in establishing computational trust, specifically how one agent can verify that another's output was genuinely produced by a claimed LLM, and not falsified or generated by a cheaper or inferior model. To address this challenge, this paper proposes a verification framework that achieves tractable asymmetric effort, where the cost to verify a computation is substantially lower than the cost to perform it. Our approach is built upon the principle of deterministic replicability, a property inherent to autoregressive models that strictly necessitates a computationally homogeneous environment where all agents operate on identical hardware and software stacks. Within this defined context, our framework enables multiple validators to probabilistically audit small, random segments of an LLM's output and it distributes the verification workload effectively. The simulations demonstrated that targeted verification can be over 12 times faster than full regeneration, with tunable parameters to adjust the detection probability. By establishing a tractable mechanism for auditable LLM systems, our work offers a foundational layer for responsible AI and serves as a cornerstone for future research into the more complex, heterogeneous multi-agent systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11068","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:54:00Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:54:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:54:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11035","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11035","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11035","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hang","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Hang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haibin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Licheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Licheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cong","familyName":"Zuo","name":"Zuo, Cong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Free-MAD: Consensus-Free Multi-Agent Debate"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T01:55:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:31:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-agent debate (MAD) is an emerging approach to improving the reasoning capabilities of large language models (LLMs). Existing MAD methods rely on multiple rounds of interaction among agents to reach consensus, and the final output is selected by majority voting in the last round. However, this consensus-based design faces several limitations. First, multiple rounds of communication increases token overhead and limits scalability. Second, due to the inherent conformity of LLMs, agents that initially produce correct responses may be influenced by incorrect ones during the debate process, causing error propagation. Third, majority voting introduces randomness and unfairness in the decision-making phase, and can degrade the reasoning performance.\n To address these issues, we propose \\textsc{Free-MAD}, a novel MAD framework that eliminates the need for consensus among agents. \\textsc{Free-MAD} introduces a novel score-based decision mechanism that evaluates the entire debate trajectory rather than relying on the last round only. This mechanism tracks how each agent's reasoning evolves, enabling more accurate and fair outcomes. In addition, \\textsc{Free-MAD} reconstructs the debate phase by introducing anti-conformity, a mechanism that enables agents to mitigate excessive influence from the majority. Experiments on eight benchmark datasets demonstrate that \\textsc{Free-MAD} significantly improves reasoning performance while requiring only a single-round debate and thus reducing token costs. We also show that compared to existing MAD approaches, \\textsc{Free-MAD} exhibits improved robustness in real-world attack scenarios."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11035","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:53:15Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:53:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:53:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11026","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.11026","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.11026","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Ziang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manasi","familyName":"Ganti","name":"Ganti, Manasi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zixian","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Zixian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Helena","familyName":"Vasconcelos","name":"Vasconcelos, Helena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qijia","familyName":"He","name":"He, Qijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ranjay","familyName":"Krishna","name":"Krishna, Ranjay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rethinking Human Preference Evaluation of LLM Rationales"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-14T01:33:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:30:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) often generate natural language rationales -- free-form explanations that help improve performance on complex reasoning tasks and enhance interpretability for human users. However, evaluating these rationales remains challenging. While recent work has relied on binary preference judgments from humans or LLM judges, such evaluations are often opaque and coarse-grained, offering limited insight into what makes one rationale better than another. In this work, we rethink preference evaluation for LLM-generated rationales by asking: (1) What attributes define good rationales? (2) Can human preferences be explained by these attributes? (3) Can attribute-based evaluation overcome the limitations of binary comparisons? We identify a set of key rationale attributes from prior literature and assess them using automatic metrics, LLM judgments, and human annotations. We then analyze two standard human preference datasets MT Bench and Chatbot Arena using SHAP to identify which attributes best explain human preference outcomes. Finally, we re-evaluate model-generated rationales using attribute-specific ELO scores, revealing more nuanced model comparisons and insights. Our findings suggest that fine-grained attribute evaluations can better characterize rationale quality and guide future research toward more interpretable and reliable evaluation practices."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in the XLLM-Reason-Plan Workshop on the Application of LLM Explainability to Reasoning and Planning at COLM 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.11026","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:53:02Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:53:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:53:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10963","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10963","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10963","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aranyak","familyName":"Acharyya","name":"Acharyya, Aranyak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carey E.","familyName":"Priebe","name":"Priebe, Carey E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hayden S.","familyName":"Helm","name":"Helm, Hayden S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Testing for LLM response differences: the case of a composite null consisting of semantically irrelevant query perturbations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T19:44:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:26:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Given an input query, generative models such as large language models produce a random response drawn from a response distribution. Given two input queries, it is natural to ask if their response distributions are the same. While traditional statistical hypothesis testing is designed to address this question, the response distribution induced by an input query is often sensitive to semantically irrelevant perturbations to the query, so much so that a traditional test of equality might indicate that two semantically equivalent queries induce statistically different response distributions. As a result, the outcome of the statistical test may not align with the user's requirements. In this paper, we address this misalignment by incorporating into the testing procedure consideration of a collection of semantically similar queries. In our setting, the mapping from the collection of user-defined semantically similar queries to the corresponding collection of response distributions is not known a priori and must be estimated, with a fixed budget. Although the problem we address is quite general, we focus our analysis on the setting where the responses are binary, show that the proposed test is asymptotically valid and consistent, and discuss important practical considerations with respect to power and computation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10963","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:51:34Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:51:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:51:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10931","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10931","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10931","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seongho","familyName":"Joo","name":"Joo, Seongho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyukhun","familyName":"Koh","name":"Koh, Hyukhun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyomin","familyName":"Jung","name":"Jung, Kyomin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Harmful Prompt Laundering: Jailbreaking LLMs with Abductive Styles and Symbolic Encoding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T18:07:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:24:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities across diverse tasks, but their potential misuse for harmful purposes remains a significant concern. To strengthen defenses against such vulnerabilities, it is essential to investigate universal jailbreak attacks that exploit intrinsic weaknesses in the architecture and learning paradigms of LLMs. In response, we propose \\textbf{H}armful \\textbf{P}rompt \\textbf{La}undering (HaPLa), a novel and broadly applicable jailbreaking technique that requires only black-box access to target models. HaPLa incorporates two primary strategies: 1) \\textit{abductive framing}, which instructs LLMs to infer plausible intermediate steps toward harmful activities, rather than directly responding to explicit harmful queries; and 2) \\textit{symbolic encoding}, a lightweight and flexible approach designed to obfuscate harmful content, given that current LLMs remain sensitive primarily to explicit harmful keywords. Experimental results show that HaPLa achieves over 95% attack success rate on GPT-series models and 70% across all targets. Further analysis with diverse symbolic encoding rules also reveals a fundamental challenge: it remains difficult to safely tune LLMs without significantly diminishing their helpfulness in responding to benign queries."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10931","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:50:49Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:50:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:50:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10913","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10913","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10913","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Hedayatnia","name":"Hedayatnia, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mostafa","familyName":"Tavassolipour","name":"Tavassolipour, Mostafa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Babak Nadjar","familyName":"Araabi","name":"Araabi, Babak Nadjar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdol-Hossein","familyName":"Vahabie","name":"Vahabie, Abdol-Hossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robustifying Diffusion-Denoised Smoothing Against Covariate Shift"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T17:27:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:23:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Randomized smoothing is a well-established method for achieving certified robustness against l2-adversarial perturbations. By incorporating a denoiser before the base classifier, pretrained classifiers can be seamlessly integrated into randomized smoothing without significant performance degradation. Among existing methods, Diffusion Denoised Smoothing - where a pretrained denoising diffusion model serves as the denoiser - has produced state-of-the-art results. However, we show that employing a denoising diffusion model introduces a covariate shift via misestimation of the added noise, ultimately degrading the smoothed classifier's performance. To address this issue, we propose a novel adversarial objective function focused on the added noise of the denoising diffusion model. This approach is inspired by our understanding of the origin of the covariate shift. Our goal is to train the base classifier to ensure it is robust against the covariate shift introduced by the denoiser. Our method significantly improves certified accuracy across three standard classification benchmarks - MNIST, CIFAR-10, and ImageNet - achieving new state-of-the-art performance in l2-adversarial perturbations. Our implementation is publicly available at https://github.com/ahedayat/Robustifying-DDS-Against-Covariate-Shift"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10913","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:50:24Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:50:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:50:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10790","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10790","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10790","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luke","familyName":"Howard","name":"Howard, Luke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GoldenTransformer: A Modular Fault Injection Framework for Transformer Robustness Research"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-13T02:52:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:15:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Transformers have become the foundation for a wide range of state--of--the--art models across natural language processing, computer vision, and other machine learning domains. Despite their widespread deployment, the robustness of these models under fault conditions remains underexplored. We present GoldenTransformer, a modular and extensible fault injection framework designed to evaluate the resiliency of Large Language Models to induced hardware faults. GoldenTransformer offers a unified Python-based platform for injecting diverse classes of faults--such as weight corruption, activation injections, and attention--level disruptions--into pretrained transformer--based models. Inspired by the GoldenEye simulator for DNNs, our framework focuses on the unique challenges of working with large transformer architectures, including considerations such as structural complexity, latent dependencies, and nonuniform layer definitions. GoldenTransformer is built atop PyTorch and HuggingFace Transformers, and it supports experiment reproducibility, metric logging, and visualization out of the box. We detail the technical design and use of GoldenTransformer and demonstrate through several example experiments on classification and generation tasks. By enabling controlled injection of faults at multiple logical and structural points in a transformer, GoldenTransformer offers researchers and practitioners a valuable tool for model robustness analysis and for guiding dependable system design in real-world LLM applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"4 Pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10790","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:47:29Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:47:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:47:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10753","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10753","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10753","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minh","familyName":"Vu","name":"Vu, Minh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian K.","familyName":"Tran","name":"Tran, Brian K.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Syed A.","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Syed A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Geigh","familyName":"Zollicoffer","name":"Zollicoffer, Geigh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nhat","familyName":"Hoang-Xuan","name":"Hoang-Xuan, Nhat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manish","familyName":"Bhattarai","name":"Bhattarai, Manish","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HalluField: Detecting LLM Hallucinations via Field-Theoretic Modeling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T23:49:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:12:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) exhibit impressive reasoning and question-answering capabilities. However, they often produce inaccurate or unreliable content known as hallucinations. This unreliability significantly limits their deployment in high-stakes applications. Thus, there is a growing need for a general-purpose method to detect hallucinations in LLMs. In this work, we introduce HalluField, a novel field-theoretic approach for hallucination detection based on a parametrized variational principle and thermodynamics. Inspired by thermodynamics, HalluField models an LLM's response to a given query and temperature setting as a collection of discrete likelihood token paths, each associated with a corresponding energy and entropy. By analyzing how energy and entropy distributions vary across token paths under changes in temperature and likelihood, HalluField quantifies the semantic stability of a response. Hallucinations are then detected by identifying unstable or erratic behavior in this energy landscape. HalluField is computationally efficient and highly practical: it operates directly on the model's output logits without requiring fine-tuning or auxiliary neural networks. Notably, the method is grounded in a principled physical interpretation, drawing analogies to the first law of thermodynamics. Remarkably, by modeling LLM behavior through this physical lens, HalluField achieves state-of-the-art hallucination detection performance across models and datasets."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10753","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:46:38Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:46:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:46:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10723","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10723","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10723","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyu","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chaoran","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Chaoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiawen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiping","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhiping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingcan","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Bingcan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ibrahim","familyName":"Khalilov","name":"Khalilov, Ibrahim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simret Araya","familyName":"Gebreegziabher","name":"Gebreegziabher, Simret Araya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingsheng","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Bingsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dakuo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Dakuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanfang","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Yanfang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianshi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tianshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziang","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Ziang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaxing","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Yaxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Toby Jia-Jun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Toby Jia-Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dark Patterns Meet GUI Agents: LLM Agent Susceptibility to Manipulative Interfaces and the Role of Human Oversight"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T22:26:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:11:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The dark patterns, deceptive interface designs manipulating user behaviors, have been extensively studied for their effects on human decision-making and autonomy. Yet, with the rising prominence of LLM-powered GUI agents that automate tasks from high-level intents, understanding how dark patterns affect agents is increasingly important. We present a two-phase empirical study examining how agents, human participants, and human-AI teams respond to 16 types of dark patterns across diverse scenarios. Phase 1 highlights that agents often fail to recognize dark patterns, and even when aware, prioritize task completion over protective action. Phase 2 revealed divergent failure modes: humans succumb due to cognitive shortcuts and habitual compliance, while agents falter from procedural blind spots. Human oversight improved avoidance but introduced costs such as attentional tunneling and cognitive load. Our findings show neither humans nor agents are uniformly resilient, and collaboration introduces new vulnerabilities, suggesting design needs for transparency, adjustable autonomy, and oversight."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10723","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:45:56Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:45:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:45:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10704","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10704","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10704","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingchen","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Xingchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruoxi","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Ruoxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hootan","familyName":"Nakhost","name":"Nakhost, Hootan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ke","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Ke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rajarishi","familyName":"Sinha","name":"Sinha, Rajarishi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sercan Ö.","familyName":"Arık","name":"Arık, Sercan Ö.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Maestro: Self-Improving Text-to-Image Generation via Agent Orchestration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T21:45:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:10:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Text-to-image (T2I) models, while offering immense creative potential, are highly reliant on human intervention, posing significant usability challenges that often necessitate manual, iterative prompt engineering over often underspecified prompts. This paper introduces Maestro, a novel self-evolving image generation system that enables T2I models to autonomously self-improve generated images through iterative evolution of prompts, using only an initial prompt. Maestro incorporates two key innovations: 1) self-critique, where specialized multimodal LLM (MLLM) agents act as 'critics' to identify weaknesses in generated images, correct for under-specification, and provide interpretable edit signals, which are then integrated by a 'verifier' agent while preserving user intent; and 2) self-evolution, utilizing MLLM-as-a-judge for head-to-head comparisons between iteratively generated images, eschewing problematic images, and evolving creative prompt candidates that align with user intents. Extensive experiments on complex T2I tasks using black-box models demonstrate that Maestro significantly improves image quality over initial prompts and state-of-the-art automated methods, with effectiveness scaling with more advanced MLLM components. This work presents a robust, interpretable, and effective pathway towards self-improving T2I generation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 7 figures, 2 tables (22 pages, 9 figures and 3 tables including references and appendices)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10704","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:45:29Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:45:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:45:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10695","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10695","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10695","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoming","familyName":"Jing","name":"Jing, Haoming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oren","familyName":"Wright","name":"Wright, Oren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"José M. F.","familyName":"Moura","name":"Moura, José M. F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yorie","familyName":"Nakahira","name":"Nakahira, Yorie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Kalman Bayesian Transformer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T21:15:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:09:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sequential fine-tuning of transformers is useful when new data arrive sequentially, especially with shifting distributions. Unlike batch learning, sequential learning demands that training be stabilized despite a small amount of data by balancing new information and previously learned knowledge in the pre-trained models. This challenge is further complicated when training is to be completed in latency-critical environments and learning must additionally quantify and be mediated by uncertainty. Motivated by these challenges, we propose a novel method that frames sequential fine-tuning as a posterior inference problem within a Bayesian framework. Our approach integrates closed-form moment propagation of random variables, Kalman Bayesian Neural Networks, and Taylor approximations of the moments of softmax functions. By explicitly accounting for pre-trained models as priors and adaptively balancing them against new information based on quantified uncertainty, our method achieves robust and data-efficient sequential learning. The effectiveness of our method is demonstrated through numerical simulations involving sequential adaptation of a decision transformer to tasks characterized by distribution shifts and limited memory resources."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the 64th IEEE Conference on Decision and Control (CDC 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10695","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:45:16Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:45:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:45:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10682","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.10682","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.10682","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vitor Hugo Galhardo","familyName":"Moia","name":"Moia, Vitor Hugo Galhardo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Igor Jochem","familyName":"Sanz","name":"Sanz, Igor Jochem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gabriel Antonio Fontes","familyName":"Rebello","name":"Rebello, Gabriel Antonio Fontes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rodrigo Duarte","familyName":"de Meneses","name":"de Meneses, Rodrigo Duarte","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Briland","familyName":"Hitaj","name":"Hitaj, Briland","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ulf","familyName":"Lindqvist","name":"Lindqvist, Ulf","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM in the Middle: A Systematic Review of Threats and Mitigations to Real-World LLM-based Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Emerging Technologies (cs.ET)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-12T20:26:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-16T00:08:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The success and wide adoption of generative AI (GenAI), particularly large language models (LLMs), has attracted the attention of cybercriminals seeking to abuse models, steal sensitive data, or disrupt services. Moreover, providing security to LLM-based systems is a great challenge, as both traditional threats to software applications and threats targeting LLMs and their integration must be mitigated. In this survey, we shed light on security and privacy concerns of such LLM-based systems by performing a systematic review and comprehensive categorization of threats and defensive strategies considering the entire software and LLM life cycles. We analyze real-world scenarios with distinct characteristics of LLM usage, spanning from development to operation. In addition, threats are classified according to their severity level and to which scenarios they pertain, facilitating the identification of the most relevant threats. Recommended defense strategies are systematically categorized and mapped to the corresponding life cycle phase and possible attack strategies they attenuate. This work paves the way for consumers and vendors to understand and efficiently mitigate risks during integration of LLMs in their respective solutions or organizations. It also enables the research community to benefit from the discussion of open challenges and edge cases that may hinder the secure and privacy-preserving adoption of LLM-based systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"37 pages, 8 figures, 13 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.10682","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-16T02:44:57Z","registered":"2025-09-16T02:44:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-16T02:44:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":50,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=doi%3A%2810.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10625+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10641+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10655+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10682+OR+10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.10687+OR+10.48550%2Farx